+#### Rolling back the WP3a pushes
+
+What the on-call person needs at 03:00, in one place. WP3a landed as TWO pushes — the migration commit alone, then the code — and each has a different rollback:
+
+- **The door itself is shut instantly without any revert:** `WEBSITE_MODULE_ENABLED=false` in `.env` + `docker compose -f docker-compose.vps.yml up -d backend` ("Shutting the door" above). Every `/api/website/v1/*` route is a 404 again within ~30 s; nothing stored is touched. Reach for this first — it covers every incident the door can cause once it is on.
+- **Rolling back the code:** `git revert <code sha>` on `main` (never a force-push — the poller latches SHAs), push, and the VPS cron poller redeploys within ~2 min; verify by artifact (`Deploy finished for <sha>` in `deploy.log`), not `git rev-parse`. The reverted image boots against the WP3a schema without complaint: the migration is additive (nullable column, unused enum values, four new tables) and **stays** — do NOT write a down-migration or drop the tables; a revert of the migration commit is neither needed nor safe once rows exist.
+- **What the seed left behind stays, and is harmless.** The code push's deploy step [6b] seeded three kinds of rows that a code revert does not remove: the `since: 3` `role_permissions` grants for the ten `website*` keys (every role is now at `defaultsVersion = 3`), the two `module_notification_settings` rows (`WEBSITE_FORM_RECEIVED`, `WEBSITE_FORMS_UNAVAILABLE`) and the ten `website_forms` rows. None of them can misbehave under the reverted code: the resolver in `packages/permissions/src/effective.ts` indexes grant rows by key and never validates a key against the registry — there is no unknown-key throw — so a `website.forms` row is simply a cell no route ever asks about; catalog conformance compares `PERMISSION_KEYS` (also reverted) against the registered modules (also reverted), never against `role_permissions`; the notification-settings and form rows are read only by the reverted module. Leave them.
+- **Re-applying later will NOT re-create those rows — and does not need to.** The seed is create-if-missing and gated on `defaultsVersion`: the grants are due only while `since (3) > role.defaultsVersion`, and every role is already at 3, so a later re-apply of the same code seeds nothing new for them — which is fine, because the rows are still there. The same holds for the settings and form rows (upserts on fixed ids/keys). If the rows WERE removed by hand in between, the grants would need either `SEED_RESET_ROLE_DEFAULTS` (dev only — the VPS compose does not carry it) or a hand-written migration; do not delete them in the first place.
+- **Order of preference:** flag off → (if the code itself is at fault for something other than the door) `git revert` the code commit → never touch the migration.
